<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/expert/jurisprudence-what-is-law">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Jurisprudence asks the question every other law lesson assumes away: <strong>what makes a
    rule "law" at all?</strong> The answers are practical, not decorative — they decide whether
    courts must enforce wicked statutes, whether judges discover law or make it, and how much
    discretion the system tolerates. Four schools have carried the argument (natural law,
    positivism, realism and Dworkin's third way), and the English legal system is where their
    consequences are visible: in how statutes are made and read, how precedent binds, who sits
    as judge, and who can afford court. This chapter covers the whole machinery — the unit's
    fifteen topics — with the jurisprudential question running underneath each section.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four schools — and the Nazi-grudge case that forced them into the open" widget="equationStep" config={jurisCfg} />

  <h2>The nature of law: what makes a rule law</h2>
  <p>
    Austin's command theory (law as the sovereign's threat-backed command) collapses on its own
    edge cases — customs, no one commanding. Hart's <em>The Concept of Law</em> (1961) rebuilt
    positivism: law is a union of primary rules (duties) and secondary rules (recognition,
    change, adjudication), with validity resting on the <em>rule of recognition</em> — an
    accepted social practice among officials, not a moral fact. The upshot is the positivist
    separation thesis: a rule's legal validity and its moral merit are separate questions, so
    "bad law is still law" — and must be obeyed or repealed lawfully. Fuller's reply (law has an
    internal morality: publicity, prospectivity, clarity — the "procedural natural law") and
    Dworkin's (law includes principles, not just rules; judges construct the law as a coherent
    moral story) keep the argument alive in every hard case.
  </p>

  <h2>Law and society</h2>
  <p>
    Law mirrors society and lags it. The mirror: the Marriage (Same Sex Couples) Act 2013 and
    the indoor smoking ban (Health Act 2006, in force 2007) codified shifts that surveys and
    public health data had already made — Parliament legislating where social consensus had
    moved. The lag: homosexuality was partially decriminalised only in 1967 (Sexual Offences
    Act), a decade after the Wolfenden Report recommended it. Roscoe Pound's phrase for the
    discipline this implies is <em>law as social engineering</em>: statutes are instruments with
    measurable social effects, and their effects feed back into reform. The jurisprudential
    point beneath the topic: if validity is a social practice (Hart), then law's content cannot
    be studied apart from the society that sustains its rule of recognition.
  </p>

  <h2>Law and morality: the Hart–Devlin argument</h2>
  <p>
    The Wolfenden Report (1957) argued that "there must remain a realm of private morality…
    which is not law's business". Lord Devlin objected: society is held together by a shared
    morality, so Attacks on it threaten the social fabric itself and may be criminalised. Hart's
    1963 reply — deploying Mill's harm principle and the imprecision of the "shared morality"
    concept — won most of the scholarly field and, eventually, the legislative one. The debate
    is the template for every culture-war legal question since: the legal status of abortion,
    assisted dying, drugs. The student's transferable skill is the structure, not the verdict:
    identify whose harm is claimed, whose morality is being enforced, and what evidence exists
    that the fabric actually tears.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the law only enforces what everyone already agrees is
    moral." Most of law is morally <em>indifferent</em> — limitation periods, service rules,
    Land Registry procedure — and some of it knowingly licenses immorality (limitation periods
    bar meritorious claims; a tortfeasor's liability may be discharged by release). The
    morality-law overlap is a subset, and the Hart–Devlin argument is about exactly where that
    subset ends.
  </p>

  <h2>Law and justice</h2>
  <p>
    Law and justice are correlated but distinct: a rule can be legally valid and unjust, which
    is precisely why the concepts need separating. Aristotle's distinction still organises the
    field: <em>distributive</em> justice (fair shares — taxation bands, quota debates) and
    <em>corrective</em> justice (repairing a wrong — damages, restitution). Formal justice means
    like cases treated alike (the rule of law's core); substantive justice asks whether the
    treatment is itself right. Sentencing law lives in the gap: the statutory aims (punishment,
    deterrence, rehabilitation, protection — Criminal Justice Act 2003, s.142) are rival
    theories of justice, and a single sentence usually buys some of each at the cost of the
    others. The jurisprudential frame: what fills the gap when rules run out is, again, theory —
    answered live in every sentencing hearing.
  </p>

  <h2>The rule of law</h2>
  <p>
    A.V. Dicey (1885) gave the classic three: no punishment without breach of law (and no
    retroactive crimes), equality before the law (officials answer to ordinary courts), and
    rights secured by the ordinary law rather than a written code. Raz later sharpened it into
    formal criteria (prospectivity, clarity, stability, accessible courts) while insisting the
    rule of law is about form, not justice; Bingham's eight principles (2010) added substance —
    adequate protection of human rights, accessible justice. The English system's test case is
    the tension between rule of law and parliamentary sovereignty: Parliament can legally pass
    retrospective or unjust statutes, so the rule of law constrains <em>how</em> law is applied
    more than <em>what</em> is enacted — one reason written constitutions entrench such
    principles and the UK relies on convention, courts and the Human Rights Act 1998.
  </p>

  <h2>Parliamentary law making</h2>
  <p>
    Statutes begin as green papers (consultation) and white papers (proposals), then run the
    gauntlet: first reading (formal), second reading (debate), committee (line-by-line), report,
    third reading — in both Commons and Lords — ending in royal assent. The Lords can delay but
    (since the Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949) not ultimately block: the Hunting Act 2004 passed
    under the 1949 Act. Parliamentary sovereignty makes the output supreme — courts cannot
    strike down an Act. The weaknesses are procedural and teachable: guillotined debates and
    unamended committee stages produce hasty law (the Dangerous Dogs Act 1991, passed in weeks,
    is the standing cautionary tale), which is why pre-legislative scrutiny and the Law
    Commission's drafting matter. Law-making here is institutional design: speed against
    scrutiny, each stage a gatekeeper.
  </p>

  <h2>Delegated legislation</h2>
  <p>
    Parliament passes enabling Acts and delegates detail: statutory instruments (roughly three
    thousand a year — many times the statute count), byelaws, and Orders in Council. The
    machinery of accountability is procedural: the Joint Committee on Statutory Instruments,
    negative resolutions (take effect unless annulled) or affirmative resolutions (need approval).
    The jurisprudential sting: most of the law citizens actually live under (fees, benefit rates,
    speed limits) is delegated, made by ministers, not the sovereign legislature — the "enabling
    act" problem, sharpened historically by the Enabling Act of 1933 in Germany, which is why
    scrutiny safeguards and ultra vires review (courts striking delegated law beyond the parent
    Act's powers) are structural necessities, not technicalities.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "delegated legislation is minor technical trivia." It is most
    of the operative law by volume, it changes daily, and ultra vires review is one of the
    busiest ways courts check executive power. Dismissing it as trivia misses where most modern
    legal change actually happens.
  </p>

  <h2>Statutory interpretation</h2>
  <p>
    Words fail; interpretation is where jurisprudence meets daily practice. The traditional
    ladder: the <em>literal rule</em> (plain meaning — Whitely v Chappell: voting as a dead man
    is not "impersonating a person entitled to vote", so no offence), the <em>golden rule</em>
    (modify absurdity — Re Sigsworth 1935: the murderer son forfeited inheritance despite the
    Administration of Estates Act's plain wording), the <em>mischief rule</em> (fix the gap the
    Act targeted — Heydon's Case 1584; Smith v Hughes 1960: prostitutes soliciting from balconies
    "in a street" fell within the Street Offences Act's mischief), and the modern
    <em>purposive approach</em> — which Pepper v Hart (1993) reinforced by admitting
    parliamentary materials in narrow cases. Judges also apply presumptions (against
    retrospectivity, against penalising without fault) and the rules of language. The
    jurisprudential confession: every approach embeds a theory — interpretation is legislation's
    continuation by other means.
  </p>

  <h2>Judicial precedent</h2>
  <p>
    Stare decisis: like cases decided alike, binding. The hierarchy is mechanical — the Supreme
    Court binds all lower courts (but not itself, since the Practice Statement 1966), the Court
    of Appeal binds all below it (with Young v Bristol Aeroplane's 1944 exceptions: its own
    conflicting decisions, decisions impliedly overruled by the Supreme Court, and per incuriam
    rulings). The craft is extracting the <em>ratio decidendi</em> — the reason for the decision
    that binds — from <em>obiter dicta</em>, which persuade but do not bind. And precedent is
    quiet law-making: R v R (1991) abolished the marital rape exemption by reinterpretation, a
    century of law changed in a judgment. The jurisprudential question is exactly the realist
    one — judges find law or make it? — and the institutional answer is: the system is designed
    for both at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Law reform</h2>
  <p>
    The system reforms itself through dedicated machinery: the Law Commission (established by
    the Law Commissions Act 1965) issues consultation papers, reports with draft Bills, and has
    historically seen around two-thirds of its recommendations implemented — a remarkable hit
    rate for unelected drafting. Its work shows the difference between reform <em>by</em> the
    system and reform <em>of</em> it: consolidation (tidying statutes), codification (the
    perennially unfinished project for English criminal law), and targeted modernisation
    (elements of the Consumer Rights Act 2015 drew on its work). Parliamentary time is the
    bottleneck: good reports wait years, which is why the Commission's independence and
    government's uptake rate are themselves rule-of-law indicators.
  </p>

  <h2>The European layer</h2>
  <p>
    EU membership (1973–2020) rewired English law-making: directly applicable regulations,
    directives transposed nationally, and the doctrine of primacy (member-state law yielding to
    EU law in scope) — a formal limit on parliamentary sovereignty the UK courts accepted while
    a member. Brexit converted, rather than deleted, that layer: the European Union (Withdrawal)
    Act 2018 preserved the body of EU-derived law as <em>retained EU law</em> (rebranded
    "assimilated law" by the 2023 Act), so thousands of rules — from workers' rights (TUPE) to
    consumer protections — continue to apply until individually revised. The jurisprudential
    residue is live: UK courts and the CJEU may diverge on identical retained-law texts, and the
    European Convention on Human Rights (a separate body, applied domestically through the Human
    Rights Act 1998) keeps a supranational voice in the system regardless of EU membership.
  </p>

  <h2>Civil courts and other dispute resolution</h2>
  <p>
    Civil justice is layered by value and complexity: the County Court for most claims, the High
    Court's three divisions (King's Bench, Chancery, Family) for bigger and specialist work, with
    the small-claims, fast and multi-track allocations setting procedure and cost exposure.
    Appeals run upward to the Court of Appeal and Supreme Court. The system's quiet majority is
    settlement: the overwhelming share of claims resolves before trial, actively pushed by
    pre-action protocols and costs sanctions — and by the alternative machinery: negotiation,
    mediation, conciliation and arbitration (binding, arbitration clauses being standard in
    commercial contracts). The jurisprudential note: "justice" here is mostly
    <em>procedural</em> — parties meeting a dispute resolver on known terms — and the design
    question is how much settlement pressure a just system may apply.
  </p>

  <h2>Criminal courts and lay people</h2>
  <p>
    All criminal cases begin in the magistrates' court, and over 90% end there — summary
    offences entirely, either-way offences unless sent up. The magistrates themselves are lay
    volunteers (around 13,000, unqualified, advised by a legally qualified adviser), deciding
    both fact and law and sentence within limits; the Crown Court above them runs with a judge
    on law and a jury of twelve on fact, convicting by unanimity or a 10–2 majority (Juries Act
    1974). The jurisprudence of lay participation is worth stating: it distributes judicial
    power to ordinary citizens, importing community conscience (juries may, in practice, judge
    the law as well as the fact) at the cost of reasons — verdicts, unlike judgments, give no
    account of themselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "juries are the normal way serious accusations are decided
    everywhere." Lay juries are historically English and now mostly common-law heritage; most of
    the world decides guilt with professional or mixed panels — a comparison the neighbouring
    chapter develops. Even in England, magistrates dispose of most cases without any jury at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Legal personnel and the judiciary</h2>
  <p>
    The profession splits into solicitors (case management, client contact, advocacy in lower
    courts; regulated by the SRA) and barristers (specialist advocacy and advisory opinions,
    self-employed in chambers; regulated by the Bar Standards Board) — a division in slow
    convergence since the Courts and Legal Services Act 1990 opened higher advocacy. The
    judiciary, since the Constitutional Reform Act 2005, is appointed by the independent
    Judicial Appointments Commission on merit, and the UK Supreme Court (2009) finally gave the
    top court a modern building and separation from the legislature (ending the Law Lords'
    seat in the House of Lords). Judicial independence is the load-bearing convention: security
    of tenure and salary, plus the self-denying rule that MPs do not attack judges personally —
    the system's whole legitimacy rests on courts not being political actors in the partisan
    sense.
  </p>

  <h2>Access to justice and funding</h2>
  <p>
    Formal rights are worth their remedy. The post-war promise — legal aid as a "pillar of the
    welfare state" (Legal Aid and Advice Act 1949) — has been pared back: LASPO 2012 removed
    whole areas (most family, welfare-benefit and housing matters) from scope, and public legal
    aid spending fell by roughly a third in the following years. The measured consequences:
    growth in litigants in person, pressure on advice charities, and early-geography problems —
    disputes that escalate because advice came too late. The system's compensations: pro bono
    schemes, law centres, charitable representation, fee remissions. The jurisprudential frame
    is Bingham's: a rights regime with no affordable route to enforcement is a statement, not a
    remedy — access to justice is the rule of law's delivery mechanism, and its decline is a
    rule-of-law statistic.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The theory returns at the edges.</strong> Every institution above is a
    jurisprudential settlement. Precedent is positivism plus realism: valid rules (the doctrine)
    that happen to be made by judges (the realist observation). The rule of law is Fuller
    institutionalised. The Human Rights Act is a constitutional compromise a theorist would
    design: rights adjudication without striking down (declarations of incompatibility leave
    sovereignty intact). And the persistent criticism that "judges make law" is answered by the
    system's own architecture — it was built for it, with appeal courts, published reasoning and
    the Practice Statement as the safety valves.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Digital justice (online courts, video hearings) changes
    procedural justice's texture — is a justice done at scale on screen the same justice?
    Parliamentary sovereignty versus entrenched rights remains the constitutional live wire
    (successive bills of rights debates). And the delegated-legislation state keeps growing:
    whether scrutiny machinery designed for three thousand instruments a year can keep pace is
    now a rule-of-law question in its own right.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hart, The Concept of Law (1961); Fuller, "Positivism and Fidelity to Law" (1958); Dworkin, Law's Empire (1986); Holmes, "The Path of the Law" (1897).</p>
